.TH CUT 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cut \- cut out selected fields of each line of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cut
.B \-b
.I list
.RB [ \-n ]
.RI [ option " ...]"
.RI [ file " ...]"
.br
.B cut
.B \-c
.I list
.RI [ option " ...]"
.RI [ file " ...]"
.br
.B cut
.B \-f
.I list
.RB [ \-d
.IR delim " |"
.BR \-w ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RI [ option " ...]"
.RI [ file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cut
writes selected parts of each input line to standard output.
Input is read from each
.I file
in turn, or from standard input if none is given or for a
.I file
of
.BR \- .
.PP
Exactly one of
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
or
.B \-f
must be given.
Its
.I list
is a comma-separated set of positions or ranges, numbered from 1:
.TP
.I N
position
.I N
only;
.TP
.IB N \-
from
.I N
to the end of the line;
.TP
.BI \- M
from 1 to
.IR M ;
.TP
.IB N \- M
from
.I N
to
.I M
inclusive.
.PP
Ranges may overlap and appear in any order; each selected byte or
field is written once, in input order.
.PP
Only single-byte characters are supported, so
.B \-c
behaves exactly like
.B \-b
and
.B \-n
has no effect.
.PP
In field mode, a line that contains no delimiter is written unchanged
unless
.B \-s
is given.
Selected fields are joined with the input delimiter (or the
.B \-\-output\-delimiter
string).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bytes= \fIlist\fP
Select bytes.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-characters= \fIlist\fP
Select characters (identical to
.BR \-b ).
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-fields= \fIlist\fP
Select fields.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delimiter= \fIdelim\fP
Use the single character
.I delim
as the field delimiter instead of tab.
.TP
.B \-w
Split fields on runs of blanks and tabs instead of a single delimiter
character; leading blanks are skipped.
Selected fields are joined with a tab.
Valid only with
.BR \-f ,
and takes precedence over
.BR \-d .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-only\-delimited
With
.BR \-f ,
do not print lines that contain no delimiter.
.TP
.B \-n
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero\-terminated
Lines are terminated by NUL bytes instead of newlines, on input and
output.
.TP
.B \-\-complement
Select every byte or field that is
.I not
in
.IR list .
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-delimiter= str
With
.BR \-f ,
join selected fields with
.IR str .
With
.B \-b
or
.BR \-c ,
write
.I str
between non-adjacent selected bytes.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A
.I file
could not be opened; the remaining files are still processed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage or list, out of memory, or a write error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print login names and shells from the password file:
.PP
.EX
$ cut \-d: \-f1,7 /etc/passwd
.EE
.PP
Print the first 8 bytes of each line:
.PP
.EX
$ cut \-b \-8 file
.EE
.PP
Print the second whitespace-separated column:
.PP
.EX
$ ps | cut \-w \-f2
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR awk (1),
.BR join (1),
.BR tr (1)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024 for
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.BR \-s .
.B \-w
follows BSD;
.BR \-z ,
.BR \-\-complement ,
.BR \-\-output\-delimiter ,
and the other long options follow GNU coreutils.
.SH BUGS
Multibyte characters are not recognised;
.B \-c
counts bytes.
.PP
.B \-s
and
.B \-d
are accepted, and ignored, with
.B \-b
and
.BR \-c .
